this is a prototype of the solar  synchronizer for the 10,000 year clock  it uses a lens to focus sunlight on a  specialized piece of wire to synchronize  the clock to noon on any sunny day and  the reason we need to do that is that  the clock itself keeps pretty good time  it has a pendulum  that's rocking back and forth like most  clocks do but that has a tendency to  drift over time and we wanted a way for  the clock to naturally what's called  phase lock to a natural cycle the most  easy to use natural cycle is that of the  Sun moving across the sky we wanted to  do this without electronics so this is a  lens and this lens is set for your  latitude at at that at an angle that is  perpendicular to the Sun at your pet  your latitude and that even though the  sun's angle changes during the course of  the year that's why we made this lens a  nice long rectangular lens so that if  when the sun's high in the summer and  low in the winter we capture all of that  and it focuses sunlight onto this narrow  band right here and it has a specialized  opening that allows just a little bit of  Sun to drop through it at first and then  more and more and then all of the Sun  and what that's doing is its heating up  a piece of wire right underneath it and  it's heating up a very specialized piece  of wire called nitinol that that wire is  a special two alloy made of nickel and  titanium also called a shape-memory  alloy or a muscle wire that when it gets  up to a certain temperature it contracts  and that contraction we can actually use  as a mechanical trigger for the clock  and that shrinking gives us a mechanical  trigger which synchronizes the clock to  noon on  sunny day
 

